Coexist with upstream iTerm2: own support directory, own bundle id.

This app runs beside the maintainer's upstream iTerm2.

Support directory. Upstream's applicationSupportDirectory appended the
executable name, which is "iTerm2" in both apps.
legacyApplicationSupportDirectory appended the literal "iTerm", which
holds Scripts/AutoLaunch.scpt, run at every launch. A script written for
upstream could then drive upstream from this app. Both methods now
append CFBundleName ("Mavericks iTerm2"), the directory shell and
pasteboard history already use. ToolNotes moves legacy notes only when
the new file is absent, so with one directory nothing moves. With no
CFBundleName (a bare executable, such as a driver test) there is no
support directory: the path component would be nil, and files would
land loose in Application Support itself.

Bundle id. Two checks named upstream's bundle id as "this app": the
hotkey window's previously-active-app check and the URL handler's
profile lookup. They now compare against this app's own bundle id. iTerm
0.x's id is dropped with them: a URL handled by another app is not this
app's to take.

